    "summary": "Legendary Other One marathon, Cosmic Charlie blaze, acoustic folk gems",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Other One and blistering Cosmic Charlie, while the acoustic set shines with Friend of the Devil, Dire Wolf and Uncle John's Band. Jerry’s guitar work is on fire throughout, and Phil’s bass drives the jam‑filled electric sets.",
    "review": "May 2, 1970 at Harpur College captured one of the Dead’s most dynamic early‑‘70 performances. The evening opened with a tight folk‑rock set – Don’t Ease Me In through Uncle John’s Band – that highlighted Garcia’s sweet vocal phrasing and Pigpen’s soulful harmonica, while Phil’s bass on Cumberland Blues roared forward. After the brief New Riders interlude, the electric side exploded: Saint Stephen segued into an immaculate Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One sandwich that turned into a marathon jam, with Jerry unleashing a blistering solo that still feels fresh today. Cosmic Charlie followed, firing up the crowd before a tight Casey Jones and two rounds of Good Lovin’ sandwiched between drum duets. The second electric half kept the momentum high with Cold Rain And Snow, a gritty It’s A Man’s World, and a raucous Dancin’ In The Street that sent everyone dancing. The third set closed on a haunting note with Morning Dew into Viola Lee Blues → Feedback, showcasing the band’s ability to swing from gentle balladry to raw improvisation in a single breath. Across all available tapes the performance feels locked‑in; even recordings with minor cuts or hiss retain the show’s energy and make this Harpur College concert a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
